---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function `solution/2` in the `Solution` module that takes two lists of integers. One list is exactly the same as the other but with one extra integer. The function should return the extra integer. You may assume that one list is exactly one element longer than the other.

The solution must determine which list is longer, sort both lists, compare them element by element, and identify the extra integer. If the shorter list is exhausted without finding a difference, the extra integer is the last element of the longer list.

Complexity: The function should handle large lists efficiently.

Edge cases:
- The lists are unordered and may contain duplicates.
- The extra integer can be at any position.
- Both lists will always differ by exactly one integer, with one list containing that integer and the other not.

---TEST---
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "finds missing number in two lists" do
    assert Solution.solution([1, 2, 3, 4], [1, 2, 4]) == 3
  end

  test "handles duplicates and unsorted lists" do
    assert Solution.solution([13, 5, 6, 2, 5], [5, 2, 5, 13]) == 6
  end

  test "missing number is at the end" do
    assert Solution.solution([10, 20, 30, 40], [10, 20, 30]) == 40
  end
end

---REFERENCE---
defmodule Solution do
  def solution(list1, list2) do
    {shorter, longer} =
      if length(list1) <= length(list2) do
        {list1, list2}
      else
        {list2, list1}
      end

    sorted_short = Enum.sort(shorter)
    sorted_long = Enum.sort(longer)

    case Enum.find_value(Enum.zip(sorted_short, sorted_long), fn {s, l} ->
           if s != l, do: l
         end) do
      nil -> List.last(sorted_long)
      found -> found
    end
  end
end
